id: pm-rl-82qw
title: The budget-exhaustion path and the sorted-run traversal were both asserted vacuously
description: "CodeRabbit review of pm-rl#12, one Major and one Trivial, sharing a root: an assertion that holds whether or not the behaviour it names is present.\n\nBUDGET EXHAUSTION was never proven. The test used a zero-permission approval, and the refusal fires at a count of zero whatever countPromotedUnderApproval returns, so the counting walk was never exercised. Its second case promoted the same generation twice and expected already promoted - a guard that runs BEFORE the budget check is reached, so that attempt never touched the budget path either. No case promoted DISTINCT candidates up to the limit.\n\nThis compounds the mutation-testing lesson from this same review. I had reported the budget refusal proven revert-proof by mutation at 31 of 32 passing. It fired because the zero-permission case trips it regardless. The count that actually bounds a recursive loop was untested.\n\nSORTED RUN TRAVERSAL was likewise undiscriminating: only one run in the fixture mapped to the evaluation environment, so the match was found under insertion order too, and removing the sort would fail nothing."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-11T00:04:53.212Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-11T21:37:26.297Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-11T00:05:12.666Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-11T00:05:12.666Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
resolution: "Both assertions discriminate rather than merely firing: the budget test promotes distinct candidates so the refusal reports a real consumed count and names the approval to extend, and the sorted-run traversal asserts the order the contamination walk depends on."
expected_result: "A refusal that fires for the right reason, verified by mutation rather than by observing that it fired."
actual_result: Each assertion was checked against a deliberately reverted implementation; a test that stayed green under mutation was rewritten until it did not.
